The graph
description: an acyclic spine, one relation kind, and a normal form
- >
  Two edge classes build every yamlover graph. **Containment** - a key holding its value - is the
  acyclic spine: every node has exactly one containing parent, and the spine is the node's identity
  path. **References** - `*` pointers and the entries `&` bookmarks create - are non-owning edges laid
  on top; they may point anywhere, an ancestor included, so the whole is a general directed graph,
  cycles allowed ([a graph, kept as written](::language:principles:graph-not-tree)).
- >
  Containment and references are **one relation kind, keyed by the child's name**: a `cain:` key
  holding a node and a `cain: *...` reference landing on the same node are the same relation, authored
  from two ends - "two parents" is two same-named child-edges into one node. A query walking up meets
  both and cannot tell which is which.
- >
  A relation may be authored **forward, reverse, or both** - the reverse spelling is the `&` bookmark
  (formerly the `~` sigil) - and both authorings produce identical normalized edges; a both-ways pair
  folds to one. The engine keeps the graph exactly as written, and `normalize` reduces a document to
  the canonical forwards-only form; queries always see the normalized view, so they cannot tell which
  way an edge was written.
- >
  Reference edges are never expanded inline - a cycle renders as a link, not an infinite tree - and on
  a filesystem a reverse-authored membership materializes as a symlink.
